{#
  containerd mirror configuration for one upstream registry, written to
  <containerd_certs_dir>/<upstream host>/hosts.toml. containerd finds this
  file by the directory name matching the registry host in an image
  reference, so manifests keep saying ghcr.io/... and the node looks at
  Harbor first without anything rewriting the reference.

  The file is read only because cluster/kubernetes.yml sets
  registry.config_path in the containerd config. Without that key containerd
  never opens this file and never says so.

  server is the fallback, and host entries are tried before it. With Harbor
  down, unreachable, or not yet installed, pulls go straight upstream — which
  is what makes it safe to deploy this before Harbor exists and what keeps a
  cluster whose registry lives inside it from being unable to start.

  override_path is required and is the setting most easily missed. By default
  containerd treats the host URL as a registry root and appends its own
  /v2/<repository>/..., which against a URL that already ends in /v2/<project>
  produces /v2/<project>/v2/<repository> and a 404 on every pull. override_path
  tells containerd the path given here is already the API root, so the request
  becomes /v2/<project>/<repository>/manifests/<tag> — the path Harbor's proxy
  cache projects serve.

  capabilities includes resolve, not just pull. containerd's own documentation
  gives public mirrors ["pull"] alone so that an untrusted mirror cannot decide
  which digest a tag names; the client asks upstream to resolve the tag and
  fetches only blobs from the mirror. That would leave the cache useless
  whenever upstream is unreachable, which is half the reason it exists. Harbor
  here is a registry the homelab runs, so it is trusted to answer both, and
  this line is what makes the cache an availability measure rather than only a
  bandwidth one.

  Pulls are anonymous. hosts.toml has no credentials field, and the
  imagePullSecrets already in the cluster are matched against the registry host
  in the image reference rather than the mirror actually contacted, so nothing
  in the existing credential path reaches Harbor. The proxy cache projects are
  public for this reason.

  Variables:
    registry_upstream_url:   Scheme and host of the upstream registry this
                             file mirrors (e.g. https://ghcr.io).
    registry_project:        Name of the Harbor proxy cache project holding
                             this upstream's cached artifacts.
    harbor_hostname:         FQDN of the Harbor service
                             (e.g. harbor.homelab.internal).
    registry_ca_path:        Full path to the homelab CA certificate on the
                             node, used to verify Harbor's TLS certificate.
#}
server = "{{ registry_upstream_url }}"

[host."https://{{ harbor_hostname }}/v2/{{ registry_project }}"]
  capabilities = ["pull", "resolve"]
  override_path = true
  ca = "{{ registry_ca_path }}"
